"""Volume 2 practice material for the AI Master Course:
- TECH_MORE (70) and PM_MORE (34): with content.TECH_BANK (30) and PM_BANK (16) they make 100 and 50.
- QUIZ: 15 multiple-choice questions per week (90), each with the answer and why.
- KEYS: a model answer for each of the 26 subject assignments; LESSON_KEYS for the 7 new lessons."""

TECH_MORE = [
 ("Python", "What is the difference between a list and a dictionary?", "A list keeps items in order and you reach them by position; a dictionary maps keys to values and you reach them by key. JSON objects become dictionaries in Python."),
 ("Python", "Why use try and except around an API call?", "Network calls fail for reasons outside your code (timeouts, rate limits, bad keys). Catching specific errors lets you retry, show a clear message or fall back instead of crashing."),
 ("Python", "What does pip install -r requirements.txt do and why pin versions?", "It installs every listed package. Pinning versions makes builds repeatable, so a new library release cannot silently break the app."),
 ("Python", "What is async and when does it help in AI apps?", "Async lets one process wait on many slow input/output calls at once, such as several model or retrieval calls, instead of waiting for each in turn. It does not speed up heavy computation."),
 ("APIs", "Difference between GET and POST?", "GET reads data and sends parameters in the URL; POST sends a body to create something or run an action. Model calls are POST requests because the prompt is in the body."),
 ("APIs", "What does HTTP 429 mean and how do you handle it?", "Too many requests: you hit a rate limit. Back off and retry with increasing delays, respect any retry-after header, and queue or batch work."),
 ("APIs", "What is the difference between 4xx and 5xx errors?", "4xx means the request was wrong (bad input, no permission); fix the request. 5xx means the server failed; retrying later can work."),
 ("APIs", "What is idempotency and why does it matter for retries?", "An idempotent request has the same effect if sent twice. Retrying a non-idempotent action such as a payment can do it twice, so use idempotency keys."),
 ("APIs", "What is streaming in an LLM API?", "The server sends the answer in pieces as it is generated. Users see text sooner, and long requests avoid time-outs."),
 ("LLM", "What is a transformer, in one sentence?", "A neural network design that uses attention to weigh how much every token in the input matters to every other token, which lets it model long-range context."),
 ("LLM", "What is attention?", "A mechanism that lets the model look at all earlier tokens and decide which ones matter most for predicting the next token."),
 ("LLM", "Why do longer prompts cost more and run slower?", "You pay per input token, and the model must process every token before answering. Output tokens are generated one at a time, so long answers are slower still."),
 ("LLM", "What is a reasoning (thinking) model and when is it worth it?", "A model that works through a problem internally before answering. Worth it for multi-step maths, code and planning; wasteful for simple lookups and short answers."),
 ("LLM", "What is effort (or reasoning budget) in modern model APIs?", "A setting that controls how much the model thinks and how thorough its answer is. Higher effort can improve hard tasks at the cost of latency and tokens."),
 ("LLM", "What is a knowledge cut-off?", "The date after which the model has no training data. Anything newer must come from retrieval or tools."),
 ("LLM", "What are open-weight models and why choose one?", "Models whose weights you can download and run yourself. Chosen for data control, offline use, customisation or cost at very high volume; you take on hosting and safety work."),
 ("LLM", "What is fine-tuning good for, and what is it bad for?", "Good for style, format and narrow repeated tasks. Bad for adding fast-changing facts, which belong in retrieval."),
 ("LLM", "What is multimodal input?", "A model that accepts images, audio or documents as well as text, such as reading a scanned invoice."),
 ("Prompting", "What is few-shot prompting?", "Putting a few worked examples in the prompt so the model copies the pattern. Good for format and tone; costs tokens on every call."),
 ("Prompting", "How do you write a good prompt for a production task?", "State the role and goal, give context and data clearly separated (for example in tags), list rules and the output format, show an example, and say what to do when unsure. Then test it on a golden set."),
 ("Prompting", "What is prompt chaining?", "Splitting a task into steps, each with its own prompt, and passing outputs forward. Easier to test and debug than one huge prompt."),
 ("Prompting", "Why separate instructions from data in a prompt?", "So text inside the data cannot be read as instructions, which reduces prompt injection and confusion."),
 ("Embeddings", "How are embeddings different from keyword search?", "Keyword search matches exact words; embeddings match meaning, so 'holiday' can find 'paid leave'. Keyword search is better for exact codes and names."),
 ("Embeddings", "What is the dimension of an embedding?", "The length of the vector, often hundreds to a few thousand numbers. Higher dimensions can hold more nuance but cost more storage and search time."),
 ("Embeddings", "Can you mix embeddings from two different models?", "No. Vectors from different models live in different spaces. Change model, re-embed everything."),
 ("RAG", "What is hybrid search?", "Combining keyword (BM25) and vector search results, usually with a weighted merge. It finds both exact terms and paraphrases."),
 ("RAG", "What is query rewriting?", "Using a model to turn a vague or conversational question into a clear search query, for example adding the subject from earlier in the chat."),
 ("RAG", "What is top-k and how do you pick k?", "The number of chunks retrieved. Too small misses answers; too big adds noise and cost. Choose by measuring recall and answer quality on the golden set; 3 to 8 is common."),
 ("RAG", "What is contextual retrieval?", "Adding a short description of where a chunk sits in its document before embedding it, so a chunk like 'It rose 3%' carries its subject."),
 ("RAG", "How do you keep a RAG index fresh?", "Re-index changed documents on a schedule or event, store document versions and dates, and remove superseded chunks."),
 ("RAG", "How do you enforce access control in RAG?", "Store access labels with each chunk and filter by the user's permissions at retrieval time, before anything reaches the model."),
 ("RAG", "When is RAG the wrong choice?", "When answers need calculation over structured data (use SQL or tools), when the knowledge is tiny and stable (put it in the prompt), or when the task is style rather than facts (prompting or fine-tuning)."),
 ("Chunking", "Fixed-size or recursive chunking: which first?", "Recursive, because it respects paragraphs and sentences. Measure recall@k before trying anything fancier."),
 ("Chunking", "How do you chunk tables?", "Keep each row with its header, or rewrite rows as sentences, so a chunk is meaningful on its own."),
 ("Vector DB", "What is an approximate nearest neighbour index?", "A structure such as HNSW that finds near-best matches very fast by not comparing against every vector. You trade a little recall for a lot of speed."),
 ("Vector DB", "What is metadata filtering?", "Restricting search to chunks whose metadata matches, such as country or department, before or during the vector search."),
 ("Vector DB", "Can PostgreSQL be a vector database?", "Yes, with the pgvector extension. Good when you already run Postgres and need joins, filters and transactions alongside vectors."),
 ("Agents", "Workflow or agent: how do you choose?", "Use a fixed workflow when the steps are known; use an agent only when the model must choose steps at run time. Workflows are cheaper and easier to test."),
 ("Agents", "What is the agent loop?", "The model decides an action, the code runs the tool, the result goes back to the model, and this repeats until the model answers or a limit is hit."),
 ("Agents", "What is a multi-agent system and its main risk?", "Several agents with different roles passing work between them. Main risks: cost, loops and errors compounding between agents; add limits and checks."),
 ("Agents", "What memory does an agent have?", "Short-term: the conversation and tool results in the context window. Long-term: notes or vectors stored outside and retrieved when needed."),
 ("Agents", "How do you evaluate an agent?", "Score task success on a set of realistic tasks, plus steps taken, tool-call accuracy, cost and safety violations. Check the trajectory, not only the final answer."),
 ("Tools", "What makes a good tool definition?", "A clear name, a description of when to use it, a strict input schema with types and limits, and outputs that are short and structured."),
 ("Tools", "How do you make tool use safe?", "Least privilege, validate inputs in code, treat outputs as data, require human approval for actions with real-world effects, and log every call."),
 ("Tools", "What happens if a tool fails?", "Return a clear error to the model as the tool result so it can try another way, and stop after a set number of failures."),
 ("MCP", "MCP server versus client?", "The server exposes tools, resources and prompts (for example a ticket system); the client is the AI application that connects to it and offers them to the model."),
 ("MCP", "What security risks does MCP add?", "Over-broad permissions, untrusted servers, and injected instructions in tool output. Approve servers, scope credentials and review actions."),
 ("Evals", "What is the difference between offline and online evaluation?", "Offline runs a fixed test set before release; online measures live traffic after release (feedback, escalations, sampled reviews). You need both."),
 ("Evals", "What metrics would you use for a RAG system?", "Retrieval recall@k, answer accuracy, citation accuracy, groundedness, correct refusal rate, latency p95 and cost per answer."),
 ("Evals", "How big should a golden set be?", "Start with 30 to 50 real questions covering the main intents and edge cases; grow it with every failure found. Coverage matters more than size."),
 ("Evals", "How do you check an LLM judge is reliable?", "Compare its scores with human labels on a sample, measure agreement, fix the rubric, and re-check whenever the judge model or prompt changes."),
 ("Evals", "What is regression testing for prompts?", "Running the full golden set after every prompt change so a fix for one case does not break others."),
 ("Hallucinations", "What is the difference between faithfulness and correctness?", "Faithfulness means the answer matches the sources given; correctness means it matches the truth. A faithful answer from an outdated document is still wrong."),
 ("Hallucinations", "How do citations help?", "They tie each claim to a source users can check, and they let you measure whether the cited source supports the claim."),
 ("Security", "What is data exfiltration through an agent?", "Injected instructions make an agent send private data out, for example through a URL or an email tool. Block outbound tools, or require approval, for untrusted contexts."),
 ("Security", "How should API keys be handled?", "Stored in a secret manager or environment variables, never in code, logs or the browser; rotated, scoped and monitored."),
 ("Security", "What is jailbreaking?", "Prompts designed to make the model ignore its safety rules. Defend with the provider's safety features, input and output checks, and limited capabilities."),
 ("Latency", "What is time to first token and how do you improve it?", "The wait before output starts. Improve it with streaming, a shorter prompt, prompt caching and a faster model."),
 ("Latency", "How does parallelism help?", "Independent steps, such as searching several sources, can run at the same time, so total time is the slowest step instead of the sum."),
 ("Cost", "What is prompt caching?", "The provider stores a repeated prompt prefix so later calls that reuse it are cheaper and faster. Put fixed content first and changing content last."),
 ("Cost", "What is the batch API used for?", "Non-urgent jobs such as nightly summaries or evals, sent together for a large discount with results returned later."),
 ("Cost", "What is model routing?", "Sending each request to the cheapest model that can handle it, for example a small model for classification and a large one for hard questions."),
 ("LLMOps", "What is a canary release for an AI feature?", "Releasing a new prompt or model to a small share of users first and comparing metrics before a full rollout."),
 ("LLMOps", "What is drift and how do you catch it?", "A change in user questions or data that lowers quality. Catch it by tracking topic mix, refusal and feedback rates and by sampling conversations weekly."),
 ("LLMOps", "Why pin model versions?", "So behaviour does not change without your eval running first. Move to a new version deliberately, after testing."),
 ("Deployment", "Why containerise an AI service?", "The same image runs the same everywhere, with fixed Python and library versions, which makes deploys and rollbacks reliable."),
 ("Deployment", "What does a health check endpoint do?", "Gives the platform a cheap way to confirm the service is up, so it can restart or stop routing traffic to an unhealthy instance."),
 ("Architecture", "Where should guardrails sit in the architecture?", "At every boundary: input, retrieval, before tools act, and output. No single layer is enough."),
 ("Responsible AI", "What is bias in an AI system and how do you test for it?", "Systematically worse outcomes for some groups. Test by slicing eval results by language, region or user group and comparing."),
 ("Responsible AI", "What should an AI feature disclose to users?", "That they are talking to AI, its limits, how to reach a human, and how their data is used."),
]

PM_MORE = [
 ("Strategy", "What makes a good first AI use case for a company?", "High volume, clear success measure, low harm when wrong, available data and an owner who wants it. Internal tools often fit before customer-facing ones."),
 ("Strategy", "How do you write an AI product vision?", "Name the user, the job they struggle with, how AI changes it, and the measurable outcome in 12 to 24 months, plus what you will not do."),
 ("Strategy", "What is a moat for an AI product when everyone uses the same models?", "Proprietary data and feedback loops, workflow integration, distribution, trust and evaluation know-how, not the model itself."),
 ("Discovery", "How do you find AI opportunities in a workflow?", "Map the workflow step by step, time each step, find language-heavy, repetitive or judgement steps, and check which have data and tolerate some error."),
 ("Discovery", "What is a Wizard of Oz test?", "Humans pretend to be the AI behind a real interface to test demand and the experience before building the model part."),
 ("Discovery", "How do you test whether users trust AI output?", "Watch them use it: do they check sources, edit, or ignore it? Track acceptance and edit rates, and ask what would make them rely on it."),
 ("Prioritisation", "How do you size the value of an AI feature?", "Volume x time saved per task x loaded cost x expected adoption, plus revenue or risk effects, minus build and run costs."),
 ("Prioritisation", "How do you say no to an AI feature request?", "Show the evidence: the problem is rare, a simpler fix exists, the data is missing or the risk is too high. Offer the smaller step you will take instead."),
 ("Metrics", "What is the difference between model metrics and product metrics?", "Model metrics measure output quality (accuracy, groundedness); product metrics measure user and business results (adoption, time saved, resolution). Good model scores do not guarantee product success."),
 ("Metrics", "Name guardrail metrics for an AI feature.", "Harmful or wrong answer rate, escalation rate, complaint rate, cost per task and latency p95. They must not get worse while the main metric improves."),
 ("Metrics", "How do you measure time saved?", "Time the task with and without the feature on matched samples, or log task durations before and after, and check quality is the same."),
 ("Metrics", "What does a good AI dashboard show?", "Volume, quality (eval score and sampled reviews), user feedback, escalation, latency, cost, and splits by version, topic and segment."),
 ("Trade-offs", "Accuracy versus coverage: how do you decide?", "Decide by the cost of a wrong answer. High-risk domains answer fewer questions with higher accuracy and refuse more; low-risk ones can answer more."),
 ("Trade-offs", "Large model or small model?", "Start with the strongest model to prove value, build the eval, then test smaller or cheaper options against the same bar."),
 ("Trade-offs", "Prompting, RAG or fine-tuning?", "Prompting first; RAG when answers depend on your own or changing knowledge; fine-tuning for consistent style or narrow tasks at scale."),
 ("Data", "What makes documents ready for RAG?", "A clear owner, current versions, no duplicates, readable text (not scans without OCR), access labels and a refresh process."),
 ("Data", "How do you get labelled data for evaluation?", "Collect real questions from logs or tickets, have experts write expected answers and sources, and add failures from production every week."),
 ("Evaluation", "Who should own the golden set?", "The PM owns what good looks like with domain experts; engineers own the harness. Changes to expected answers need review."),
 ("Evaluation", "What is a release bar?", "The minimum scores a version must meet to ship, agreed in advance, for example 90 percent accuracy and 100 percent correct refusal on unsafe questions."),
 ("Responsible AI", "How would you handle a biased output reported by a user?", "Acknowledge, reproduce, assess impact, contain if needed, fix the data or prompt, add the case to the eval and report the change."),
 ("Responsible AI", "When must a human stay in the loop?", "When an error is costly or irreversible: money, health, legal, hiring or account changes. The AI can draft; a person decides."),
 ("Governance", "What goes into an AI risk register?", "Each risk (hallucination, data leak, injection, bias, cost overrun, outage), likelihood, impact, owner, mitigation and how it is monitored."),
 ("Governance", "How do privacy rules such as India's DPDP Act affect an AI product?", "You need a lawful basis or consent for personal data, purpose limits, minimisation, security, retention limits and a way to handle user requests. Check with legal for specifics."),
 ("Stakeholders", "How do you explain AI uncertainty to executives?", "Use numbers from the eval (for example 92 of 100 answers correct), show examples of the failures, and explain the safeguards and the plan to improve."),
 ("Stakeholders", "Legal wants to block the launch. What do you do?", "Understand the specific risk, show the controls and eval evidence, propose a narrower or staged launch, and agree the conditions to expand."),
 ("Business case", "How do you price an AI feature?", "Base it on value delivered and on cost to serve: include tokens per task at expected usage, set limits or tiers for heavy users, and watch margin."),
 ("Business case", "What hidden costs do AI products have?", "Evaluation and labelling, monitoring, human review, prompt maintenance, provider price changes, retries and support for wrong answers."),
 ("Launch", "What is in an AI launch checklist?", "Eval above the bar, red-team results, monitoring and alerts, rollback switch, human handoff, user disclosure, support briefed, and a staged rollout plan."),
 ("Launch", "How do you run an A/B test for an AI feature?", "Randomise users, pick a primary metric and guardrails up front, run long enough for the novelty effect to fade, and check results by segment."),
 ("Case", "Adoption of your AI assistant is 10 percent after a month. What do you do?", "Find out why: discoverability, trust, quality or fit with the workflow. Look at usage funnels and talk to non-users, then fix the biggest blocker first."),
 ("Case", "Costs doubled last month. What do you do?", "Break cost down by feature, prompt version and user; look for longer prompts, retries, heavy users or a model change; apply caching, limits or routing; re-run the eval."),
 ("Case", "A competitor launches an AI feature. Should you copy it?", "Only if it solves a real problem for your users better than your current plan. Check the evidence and your data advantage, then decide."),
 ("Case", "Design a metric framework for an AI meeting summariser.", "North Star: meetings with summaries used. Quality: rep edit rate, missed action items. Product: weekly active users. Business: time saved and CRM data completeness. Guardrails: privacy incidents, latency."),
 ("Case", "Your AI tutor gives a wrong maths answer to a student. Walk through your response.", "Contain (add a check or disable the topic), investigate the cause, fix, add the case to the eval, communicate with the student and teachers, and track the rate going forward."),
]

